Subject: virtio/fs, hvf: Rosetta for Linux share, per-task TSO, per-device sync drain

Rosetta for Linux checks that it runs in a Rosetta-enabled VM with
ioctls on its own binary: a key it compares with a string embedded in
the binary (_IOR('a', nr, char[69])), the AOT cache config
(_IOR('a', 0x23, char[128])) and, when the guest kernel cannot switch
the memory model, a TSO request (_IO('a', 0x24)). Fs::set_rosetta()
(FsDevice::set_rosetta()) makes a share of the RosettaLinux directory
answer them: the key is read from the shared rosetta binary at setup,
the AOT config is all zeros (no rosettad: containers live in other
network namespaces, so its abstract socket is out of reach) and the TSO
request succeeds.

hvf: the vendor call 0xC600_4B10 (x1 = 1 TSO, 0 default) sets
ACTLR_EL1.EnTSO on the calling vCPU (HV_SYS_REG_ACTLR_EL1, macOS 15+).
The guest kernel makes it on context switch only for tasks that asked
for TSO, so native code keeps the weak model. KATAMARAN_TSO=0 refuses
the call, for comparison.

virtio/fs: KAT_FS_DRAIN was registered once per device, so with a
second device the last one activated took every drain and requests on
the other device's queues were never answered (the guest hung in
request_wait_answer on /Users). Devices now go into one registry, and
the guest names the queue by index and descriptor table address (x2),
which picks the device; a guest without the address is served only
when there is a single device.

---
